text:
Rina: quick design check  we're considering supporting anchor-style permalinks for deeper nav items (e.g. /workspaces/alpha#models:model-42) vs canonical path segments. thoughts on UX and accessibility tradeoffs?
Owen: anchors are nice for intra-page jumps but fragile if we later change nav structure. canonical paths (/workspaces/:id/models/:modelId) are more robust and shareable. either way, the nav must reveal the linked item + provide clear orientation.
Diego: agreement on canonical paths. implementation-wise router mount should derive nav state from the path and expose a stable nav id we can focus. also we need to avoid forcing the main page to scroll. put focus into the nav container, not the page body.
Marta: accessibility: when a deep-link causes a hidden group to open, we should announce the change politely and set a clear focus target. proposal: an offscreen aria-live region announces "Navigation: Models expanded, Model 42 selected" but only for external arrivals (not intra-app clicks) to avoid noise.
Rina: for visual orientation I'd like a subtle visual cue in the nav (pulse ring for 1s) and a small ephemeral banner near top-left: "Linked: Models revealed" with a tiny "revert" link. keep it low-key.
Owen: caution on animations — respect prefers-reduced-motion. also keep any visual pulses within the nav so main content doesn't reflow.
Diego: focus management detail: make the selected item programmatically focusable via `tabindex="-1"` then call `element.focus({preventScroll: true})`. after that call `navContainer.scrollTop = computeNearestScroll(el)` or `el.scrollIntoView({ block: 'nearest' })` scoped to the nav so the page viewport doesn't jump.
Marta: keyboard flow we should validate: Tab -> Land on "Skip to main" link if present -> Shift+Tab back into nav -> Arrow keys to navigate groups and items -> deep-linked item appears and receives programmatic focus -> Enter to activate. test with VoiceOver + NVDA.
Rina: routing question: should we ever encode nav open state in querystring like `?open=models`? pros: easy to restore UI state. cons: noise in URLs. I prefer deriving state from the canonical path and reserve query flags for experimental toggles.
Owen: agree. Also consider a short-lived referrer flag in sessionStorage to control whether we show the ephemeral banner when arriving from an external site.
Diego: edge-case: if user previously collapsed the group manually, deep-link should override on first load (explicit intent) but not on subsequent navigations once they toggle it manually. persist manual toggles per workspace in localStorage. add an undo affordance on the banner.
Marta: aria details: use `role="navigation"` + nested `ul` lists, not role=tree, so we avoid complex keyboard trapping. add `aria-current="true"` (or `aria-current="page"`) on the selected link. ensure the breadcrumb has `aria-label` for screen readers like "Breadcrumbs: Workspace, Models, Model 42".
Rina: sample router hook snippet suggestion:
```
// on route mount
const { modelId } = useParams();
if (modelId) {
  expandNavGroup('models');
  const target = document.querySelector(`[data-nav-id="model-${modelId}"]`);
  target?.setAttribute('tabindex', '-1');
  target?.focus({ preventScroll: true });
  // scroll only the nav container
  navContainer?.scrollIntoView({ block: 'nearest' });
}
```
Owen: QA note  don't forget reduced-motion variant, screen reader announcement tests, and a regression that ensures main page scroll doesn't change on deep-link.
Diego: A11yBot, can you add checklist items?
A11yBot: added checklist entries A11Y-500 "nav reveal for canonical path deep-links", A11Y-501 "aria-live announcement only for external arrivals", A11Y-502 "nav focus with preventScroll + confined scrollIntoView".
Marta: action items: (1) router mount -> derive nav state (Owen), (2) implement focus + preventScroll + confined scroll (Diego), (3) ephemeral banner + undo + session referrer guard (Rina), (4) a11y announcements + breadcrumbs SR label (Marta). I'll update the Figma and attach a keyboard gif + reduced-motion frames. tagging @Owen @Diego @Marta :sparkles:
